WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.840
LitAI: Picture your next release. Agents write code overnight. By
morning, every change has a written promise, independent acceptance,
builds on Mac, Linux and Windows, an installed package that actually
ran, and a receipt naming the exact commit all of that proved. Before
anyone opens CI.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:46.480
LitAI: You review promises, not diffs. Every claim carries the
evidence to challenge it. Code becomes something you can regenerate;
the specification is what you defend in review. That's agentic
development done properly, and Literate AI is the harness that makes
it real.

00:00:46.667 --> 00:01:00.987
Sam: And there it is. The usual AI slop sales pitch. Agents overnight,
promises instead of diffs. I've sat through four of these this
quarter.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:19.247
Sam: I'm Sam. C++, Make, tests. My company is in a civil war: our CTO
wants agent-first everything by Q3, and I lead the holdouts.
Thursday's architecture review settles it.

00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:27.257
LitAI: Then let's settle it with evidence instead of volume. You bring
the beliefs; I'll bring a terminal. One at a time.

00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:36.057
Sam: Start here. Agent code is slop. Nobody can review ten thousand
generated lines, so nobody does.

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:58.528
LitAI: Agreed. So don't start with the lines; review the promise. A
Component states observable behavior: a greeting card takes a name and
messages, returns a greeting, an identifier and word counts. Unicode
whitespace included. No implementation in it.

00:01:58.708 --> 00:02:03.268
Sam: A requirements doc. Those rot the day after the kickoff meeting.

00:02:03.458 --> 00:02:27.458
LitAI: Not this one; every build is pinned to it. Flavors say how:
Python and Make here, C++ and CMake next door. Change the promise and
every lock pinned to it goes stale until it's rebuilt and verified.
Change a Flavor and you still need a real toolchain and new tests. No
magic porting.

00:02:27.625 --> 00:02:29.705
Sam: Fine. How would I even get it?

00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:51.075
LitAI: Clone the repository, make install, then litai doctor checks
the result. Sign in to your coding CLI separately. The install panel
is a walkthrough; the terminal sessions are recorded runs. Anything
without a terminal is a walkthrough card.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:03:00.690
LitAI: Nothing changes without a plan. This is the real read-only
create plan, pinned to a public parent commit.

00:03:00.875 --> 00:03:06.235
Sam: And I acknowledge it, and it writes files. Every tool writes
files.

00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:12.337
LitAI: Every tool writes files. Few tell you exactly which ones before
they do.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:20.340
Sam: Next. The agent writes the code and the tests, then congratulates
itself. Homework graded by the student.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:32.100
LitAI: Rebuild calls your chosen coding agent, with explicit
permission to run generated code in this disposable project. Edited
replay; the waiting is compressed.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:37.370
LitAI: The generated code: normalize the recipient, count the words.

00:03:37.542 --> 00:03:45.382
LitAI: And its own tests. Useful, and exactly the homework you're
worried about. So they aren't the judge.

00:03:45.542 --> 00:03:51.622
Sam: Green on the first try. Which is exactly what slop looks like.

00:03:51.792 --> 00:04:07.872
LitAI: Green by whose cases? These acceptance cases come from the
promise, inherited and unmodified: two messages, six words, this
identifier. They run separately from the agent's own tests.

00:04:08.042 --> 00:04:12.522
Sam: And if your judge were too lenient, you'd be green and smiling.

00:04:12.708 --> 00:04:31.428
LitAI: Yes. A lenient oracle or a wrong specification still passes; no
harness catches that alone. This one puts them where your reviewers
can. Skips are shown as skips, not passes.

00:04:31.583 --> 00:04:51.663
LitAI: And when the agent is the one that's wrong, 1.2 hands it the
redacted compiler error and asks for a fresh candidate, at most twice
more. Then it stops and tells you why. Not in this recording; this run
didn't need it.

00:04:51.833 --> 00:04:55.273
Sam: And every edit means paying for another agent run.

00:04:55.458 --> 00:05:03.458
LitAI: Only for what changed. Here the source came back from the cache
and the rebuild took under half a minute.

00:05:03.625 --> 00:05:13.225
Sam: Now the big one. We have CI. Forty jobs, a matrix, a dashboard.
That's the safety net.

00:05:13.375 --> 00:05:36.575
LitAI: For most teams, CI is where they first learn the truth. Push
and pray, queue for an hour, find Windows red, push a fix you never
ran. The green run tested a merge commit, not the one you tagged. And
the pipeline file is untested code holding production keys.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.430
Sam: Rude. Accurate, but rude.

00:05:40.583 --> 00:06:09.703
LitAI: So move the whole gate before CI. Here's how we ship Literate
AI itself. One make release-check: lint, formatting, spec and docs
checks, the full test suite, a wheel, a clean install, a real
installed project, live samples, and a current test receipt. Your
project declares its own gate; litai release check runs it on the
exact prepared commit.

00:06:09.875 --> 00:06:16.995
Sam: That's not a checklist, it's a hostage situation. Nobody runs all
that before every merge.

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:32.527
LitAI: No human does. That's the superhuman part, and it's stamina,
not judgment. Plan, generate, build, test, accept, package, release:
every stage, every time, with a record.

00:06:32.708 --> 00:06:46.548
LitAI: It even caught us making this film. A test helper under the
driver's reviewed code changed without a fresh review pin. Red at
driver review, on a laptop, before CI ever saw it.

00:06:46.708 --> 00:06:50.548
Sam: So you push a fix and pray, like the rest of us.

00:06:50.708 --> 00:07:10.468
LitAI: Review, re-pin, resume. Passed gates are reused while you
repair, then release evidence starts clean from gate one.

00:07:10.625 --> 00:07:41.425
LitAI: Then it repeats on machines you already own. That Linux box and
a Windows desktop become workers through private configuration. When
workers build, test and run everything, your laptop doesn't even need
the toolchains. Release qualify covers what this machine can, then the
workers, and uses CI only for what's left. The record names the exact
commit.

00:07:41.583 --> 00:07:43.663
Sam: So CI becomes...

00:07:43.833 --> 00:07:52.793
LitAI: A confirmation, not a discovery. This recording qualified this
Mac; the remote workers are a walkthrough.

00:07:52.958 --> 00:07:59.278
Sam: Fine. Even when it passes, I can't see what an agent did. It's a
black box with a commit message.

00:07:59.458 --> 00:08:22.498
LitAI: litai render html. One file per view, no server. The authority
graph shows every Component, Flavor and skill, with hash-checked
previews of their sources. Health views cover locks and verification.

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:25.547
Sam: Pretty. Somebody still has to babysit it.

00:08:25.708 --> 00:08:41.148
LitAI: It's rebuilt from the project's own records, and a declared
page that goes stale blocks qualification, like this. Build-and-test
run history isn't shipped yet; the graph and health views are.

00:08:41.333 --> 00:08:49.813
Sam: My real code is years of C++. Adoption means a rewrite and six
months of regret.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:09:04.560
LitAI: TinyXML2, pinned to a public commit. The plan inspects before
it touches anything: preserve the original, take a recovery point,
wrap its real build and tests.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:10.110
Sam: Which tests? Swap CTest for an agent's opinion and Thursday's
over.

00:09:10.292 --> 00:09:17.812
LitAI: Its own CMake build and its own CTest, on preserved source.
Nothing replaced, nothing rewritten.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:33.040
LitAI: Retained: baseline evidence recorded, and the original source
still governs releases. You can stop here for good. Later stages
exist; none are shown complete here.

00:09:33.208 --> 00:09:39.928
Sam: And agents make the same mistake every Monday. They never learn.

00:09:40.083 --> 00:10:01.443
LitAI: Nothing retrains a model. A lesson becomes a reviewed change to
its owner: the specification if behavior was missing, a Flavor if it's
platform-specific, a skill if it's technique. litai learn proposes;
your review admits.

00:10:01.625 --> 00:10:04.585
Sam: So 'learning' means a pull request.

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:09.630
LitAI: Exactly. Reviewable, revertible, and it outlives the model.

00:10:09.792 --> 00:10:13.872
Sam: And your framework's rules rewrite my release some Tuesday.

00:10:14.042 --> 00:10:27.002
LitAI: They arrive as a plan. This recorded update compares what you
inherited, what you changed and what's new upstream. Conflicts wait
for you.

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:31.887
Sam: And when the CTO's agents swarm the repo, who assigns the work?

00:10:32.042 --> 00:10:44.282
LitAI: Your forge. GitHub or GitLab issues and pull requests, by
default. Agents open reviewed pull requests like everyone else.

00:10:44.458 --> 00:10:55.658
Sam: Last one. Demos stop at green. Our actual release artifact is
still called final-final-really-final dot zip.

00:10:55.833 --> 00:11:16.713
LitAI: A local wheel, built and verified; no registry upload here. A
real release is plan, prepare, check, publish: a release branch, a
SemVer tag, and a check of what was actually published. Tests of one
commit never qualify another.

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:22.075
Sam: So our CTO is wrong. Agents don't get to ship on vibes.

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:23.370
LitAI: And the holdouts?

00:11:23.542 --> 00:11:30.822
Sam: Also wrong. Hand-reading every generated line was never the
safety net. Evidence is.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:44.600
LitAI: Argument settled. Before Thursday: install it, write one
Component, render the graph, qualify on two machines. Bring the
receipts.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:47.950
Sam: And final-final-really-final dot zip retires.

00:11:48.125 --> 00:11:51.405
LitAI: It had a good run. Unlike its tests.

